1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,645
<font color="#FFFFFF">Fake audio detectors work well on the data they
were trained on,</font>

2
00:00:04,725 --> 00:00:09,199
<font color="#FFFFFF">but their accuracy collapses when they meet audio
from a new dataset.</font>

3
00:00:09,279 --> 00:00:13,094
<font color="#FFFFFF">Fine-tuning on the new data then makes them forget
the old,</font>

4
00:00:13,174 --> 00:00:15,801
<font color="#FFFFFF">a problem called catastrophic forgetting.</font>

5
00:00:15,881 --> 00:00:21,214
<font color="#FFFFFF">This ICML 2023 paper introduces Regularized
Adaptive Weight Modification, or RAWM,</font>

6
00:00:21,294 --> 00:00:27,155
<font color="#FFFFFF">a continual learning method that adapts how it
modifies network weights based on the ratio</font>

7
00:00:27,235 --> 00:00:29,135
<font color="#FFFFFF">of genuine to fake utterances,</font>

8
00:00:29,215 --> 00:00:35,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">and adds a regularization term so the model
remembers the feature distribution of earlier datasets.</font>

9
00:00:35,750 --> 00:00:37,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">Without replaying any past samples,</font>

10
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:43,921
<font color="#FFFFFF">RAWM cuts forgetting to roughly one tenth of naive
fine-tuning and even generalizes beyond</font>

11
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:47,750
<font color="#FFFFFF">audio to speech emotion recognition and image recognition.</font>

12
00:00:48,130 --> 00:00:55,165
<font color="#FFFFFF">Fake audio detection has become critical as speech
synthesis and voice conversion produce human-like speech.</font>

13
00:00:55,245 --> 00:00:58,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">Detectors perform well on their own dataset,</font>

14
00:00:58,144 --> 00:01:03,071
<font color="#FFFFFF">but their equal error rate rises dramatically on
audio from another dataset.</font>

15
00:01:03,151 --> 00:01:06,035
<font color="#FFFFFF">The obvious fix, fine-tuning on the new data,</font>

16
00:01:06,115 --> 00:01:09,461
<font color="#FFFFFF">causes the network to forget what it learned before.</font>

17
00:01:09,541 --> 00:01:12,557
<font color="#FFFFFF">Earlier remedies require replaying old samples,</font>

18
00:01:12,637 --> 00:01:16,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is impractical when the original data is
inaccessible.</font>

19
00:01:16,890 --> 00:01:20,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">Existing weight-modification methods like OWM treat
every input the same when constraining updates.</font>

20
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:24,090
<font color="#FFFFFF">But in fake audio detection,</font>

21
00:01:24,170 --> 00:01:27,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">genuine speech tends to look similar from one
dataset to the next,</font>

22
00:01:27,810 --> 00:01:31,370
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the fake speech varies.</font>

23
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:35,010
<font color="#FFFFFF">That regularity is an opportunity: the direction of
a weight update should adapt to how</font>

24
00:01:35,090 --> 00:01:38,650
<font color="#FFFFFF">much of a batch is genuine versus fake.</font>

25
00:01:38,730 --> 00:01:42,290
<font color="#FFFFFF">At the same time,</font>

26
00:01:42,370 --> 00:01:45,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">some datasets collect genuine audio under acoustic
conditions so different that a naive rule backfires,</font>

27
00:01:46,010 --> 00:01:49,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">motivating an extra safeguard.</font>

28
00:01:49,950 --> 00:01:53,513
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors contribute Regularized Adaptive Weight
Modification.</font>

29
00:01:53,593 --> 00:01:57,156
<font color="#FFFFFF">It has two essential steps.</font>

30
00:01:57,236 --> 00:02:00,799
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

31
00:02:00,879 --> 00:02:04,442
<font color="#FFFFFF">adaptive weight modification introduces an extra
projector that adjusts the update direction</font>

32
00:02:04,522 --> 00:02:08,085
<font color="#FFFFFF">according to the ratio of classes with similar
feature distribution,</font>

33
00:02:08,165 --> 00:02:11,728
<font color="#FFFFFF">such as genuine utterances, to the others.</font>

34
00:02:11,808 --> 00:02:15,371
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, a regularization term, inspired by learning
without forgetting,</font>

35
00:02:15,451 --> 00:02:19,014
<font color="#FFFFFF">keeps the new inference distribution close to the old one.</font>

36
00:02:19,094 --> 00:02:22,657
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method needs no previous samples,</font>

37
00:02:22,737 --> 00:02:26,300
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the authors show it transfers to speech emotion
recognition and image recognition.</font>

38
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:30,370
<font color="#FFFFFF">RAWM starts from the orthogonal projector P of OWM,</font>

39
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:34,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">which points the update away from the subspace
spanned by previous inputs.</font>

40
00:02:34,220 --> 00:02:37,910
<font color="#FFFFFF">On top of it,</font>

41
00:02:37,990 --> 00:02:41,680
<font color="#FFFFFF">the method builds a second projector Q that is
orthogonal to P and scaled by the ratio</font>

42
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:45,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">beta of genuine to fake utterances in the batch.</font>

43
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:49,220
<font color="#FFFFFF">The two projectors are normalized and combined into
a modified direction R,</font>

44
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:52,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">so when a batch is mostly genuine the update leans
toward preserving old knowledge,</font>

45
00:02:53,070 --> 00:02:56,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">and otherwise it leans toward learning the new data.</font>

46
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:00,530
<font color="#FFFFFF">For datasets recorded under very different conditions,</font>

47
00:03:00,610 --> 00:03:04,300
<font color="#FFFFFF">a regularization term treats the frozen pre-trained
model as a teacher and forces the</font>

48
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:08,070
<font color="#FFFFFF">fine-tuned student to match its softened outputs,</font>

49
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:11,840
<font color="#FFFFFF">remembering the old inference distribution.</font>

50
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:15,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially, none of this replays past samples.</font>

51
00:03:15,990 --> 00:03:20,227
<font color="#FFFFFF">Experiments run on four fake audio datasets in a
continual-learning sequence: ASVspoof 2019 LA as the source,</font>

52
00:03:20,307 --> 00:03:24,545
<font color="#FFFFFF">then ASVspoof 2015, the Voice Conversion Challenge
2020 set,</font>

53
00:03:24,625 --> 00:03:28,862
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the In-the-Wild dataset.</font>

54
00:03:28,942 --> 00:03:33,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each is a distinct acoustic and linguistic condition,</font>

55
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:37,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">with In-the-Wild being real-world deepfakes of
public figures.</font>

56
00:03:37,577 --> 00:03:41,815
<font color="#FFFFFF">Detection quality is reported as Equal Error Rate.</font>

57
00:03:41,895 --> 00:03:46,132
<font color="#FFFFFF">To show breadth,</font>

58
00:03:46,212 --> 00:03:50,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">the method is also evaluated on speech emotion
recognition and on the CLEAR-10 image recognition benchmark.</font>

59
00:03:50,830 --> 00:03:58,277
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline finding is that RAWM cuts catastrophic
forgetting to roughly one tenth of naive fine-tuning,</font>

60
00:03:58,357 --> 00:04:01,718
<font color="#FFFFFF">while also halving the error on the new dataset.</font>

61
00:04:01,798 --> 00:04:05,015
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across two-dataset and four-dataset sequences,</font>

62
00:04:05,095 --> 00:04:11,682
<font color="#FFFFFF">it achieves the lowest equal error rate on both old
and new datasets compared with mainstream</font>

63
00:04:11,762 --> 00:04:14,621
<font color="#FFFFFF">continual learning methods including EWC,</font>

64
00:04:14,701 --> 00:04:17,704
<font color="#FFFFFF">LwF, OWM, and the fake-audio-specific DFWF.</font>

65
00:04:17,784 --> 00:04:21,431
<font color="#FFFFFF">With the regularization coefficient set to one half,</font>

66
00:04:21,511 --> 00:04:24,514
<font color="#FFFFFF">giving equal attention to old and new data,</font>

67
00:04:24,594 --> 00:04:29,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">RAWM keeps error low across all four datasets even
as a baseline collapses.</font>

68
00:04:30,270 --> 00:04:32,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">An ablation separates the two components.</font>

69
00:04:32,911 --> 00:04:36,889
<font color="#FFFFFF">When old and new datasets share a similar feature
distribution,</font>

70
00:04:36,969 --> 00:04:40,175
<font color="#FFFFFF">adaptive weight modification does most of the work,</font>

71
00:04:40,255 --> 00:04:42,558
<font color="#FFFFFF">and removing it sharply raises error.</font>

72
00:04:42,638 --> 00:04:46,617
<font color="#FFFFFF">When the datasets are recorded under very different
conditions,</font>

73
00:04:46,697 --> 00:04:50,804
<font color="#FFFFFF">the regularization term becomes the key to
overcoming forgetting.</font>

74
00:04:50,884 --> 00:04:53,252
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across the full four-dataset sequence,</font>

75
00:04:53,332 --> 00:04:58,276
<font color="#FFFFFF">removing adaptive weight modification hurts more
than removing regularization,</font>

76
00:04:58,356 --> 00:05:02,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">so it is the primary driver, with regularization a
valuable complement.</font>

77
00:05:03,230 --> 00:05:07,351
<font color="#FFFFFF">In numbers: forgetting drops to about one tenth of
fine-tuning,</font>

78
00:05:07,431 --> 00:05:10,018
<font color="#FFFFFF">and new-dataset error to about one half.</font>

79
00:05:10,098 --> 00:05:13,952
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the few-sample regime with only one hundred new
samples,</font>

80
00:05:14,032 --> 00:05:21,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">RAWM scores an equal error rate of zero point nine
two on the old set and zero point three one on the new,</font>

81
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:23,554
<font color="#FFFFFF">far ahead of fine-tuning's near eight.</font>

82
00:05:23,634 --> 00:05:29,422
<font color="#FFFFFF">On speech emotion recognition it reaches about
forty-two percent accuracy on MSP-Podcast</font>

83
00:05:29,502 --> 00:05:31,689
<font color="#FFFFFF">and fifty-four percent on IEMOCAP,</font>

84
00:05:31,769 --> 00:05:35,023
<font color="#FFFFFF">the best of all continual learning methods tested.</font>

85
00:05:35,103 --> 00:05:38,090
<font color="#FFFFFF">The optimal regularization weight is one half.</font>

86
00:05:38,470 --> 00:05:45,031
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple: you can teach a fake audio
detector new datasets without it forgetting the old,</font>

87
00:05:45,111 --> 00:05:48,062
<font color="#FFFFFF">and without keeping any of the old data around.</font>

88
00:05:48,142 --> 00:05:53,671
<font color="#FFFFFF">RAWM does this by making the weight update adapt to
how genuine-heavy each batch is and</font>

89
00:05:53,751 --> 00:05:57,540
<font color="#FFFFFF">by regularizing the model to remember its previous
behavior.</font>

90
00:05:57,620 --> 00:06:02,698
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because the underlying regularity, some classes
staying similar across datasets,</font>

91
00:06:02,778 --> 00:06:04,310
<font color="#FFFFFF">appears in many problems,</font>

92
00:06:04,390 --> 00:06:09,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">the same recipe extends to speech emotion
recognition and image recognition.</font>
